1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,885
By Bankrate's estimate, Americans are
still sitting on about $27 billion in

2
00:00:05,885 --> 00:00:10,018
gift cards, vouchers and store
credit they haven't used.

3
00:00:10,708 --> 00:00:12,229
Some of it may be yours.

4
00:00:12,536 --> 00:00:15,727
And if that money never
gets spent, who keeps it?

5
00:00:16,516 --> 00:00:20,682
That figure comes from
a survey run online by

6
00:00:20,682 --> 00:00:24,386
YouGov in August 2024 with 2,373 adults.

7
00:00:24,596 --> 00:00:27,920
43 percent said they're
holding at least one.

8
00:00:28,291 --> 00:00:32,836
It's an estimate, not a count, and
Bankrate doesn't publish how it calculated

9
00:00:32,836 --> 00:00:36,436
it, which means it tells you
the scale, not the exact amount.

10
00:00:37,025 --> 00:00:38,628
We don't have to guess.

11
00:00:39,044 --> 00:00:42,069
Starbucks, and Darden,
the company behind Olive

12
00:00:42,069 --> 00:00:44,836
Garden, explain it in
their annual filings.

13
00:00:45,233 --> 00:00:49,377
And the answer is stranger
than "the store just keeps it."

14
00:00:53,399 --> 00:00:55,076
Start with Starbucks.

15
00:00:55,366 --> 00:00:59,223
In the US and its other
company-operated markets, Starbucks

16
00:00:59,223 --> 00:01:02,491
cards have no expiration
date and no service fees.

17
00:01:03,027 --> 00:01:05,930
Nothing quietly eats the balance.

18
00:01:06,152 --> 00:01:07,662
The money just waits.

19
00:01:08,190 --> 00:01:12,642
And yet Starbucks still turns part
of that waiting money into revenue.

20
00:01:13,139 --> 00:01:17,347
The accounting name for it is
breakage: the share of card value

21
00:01:17,347 --> 00:01:20,487
a company doesn't expect
anyone to ever redeem.

22
00:01:21,797 --> 00:01:25,117
But how do you book money
that can still be spent?

23
00:01:25,539 --> 00:01:28,088
You can't wait for the card to expire.

24
00:01:28,307 --> 00:01:29,307
It won't.

25
00:01:29,545 --> 00:01:31,373
So Starbucks looks backward.

26
00:01:31,644 --> 00:01:36,596
Its filing says it builds redemption rates
from historical patterns in each market,

27
00:01:36,596 --> 00:01:41,071
including when a card was activated or
reloaded, and through which channel.

28
00:01:41,490 --> 00:01:46,322
From that history, it estimates how
much of the money is never coming back.

29
00:01:46,735 --> 00:01:47,984
Here's the clever part.

30
00:01:48,236 --> 00:01:51,126
It doesn't book that amount in one go.

31
00:01:51,463 --> 00:01:55,859
It books it over time, in proportion
to the cards that do get redeemed.

32
00:01:56,353 --> 00:01:59,223
Picture someone tapping
a card for a latte.

33
00:01:59,478 --> 00:02:03,904
As that balance gets spent, a matching
sliver of the money the company

34
00:02:03,904 --> 00:02:07,381
doesn't expect anyone to
spend moves over into revenue.

35
00:02:07,932 --> 00:02:11,540
One phrase in that same
filing will matter later.

36
00:02:11,833 --> 00:02:15,425
The estimate includes "remittance
to government agencies

37
00:02:15,425 --> 00:02:18,375
under unclaimed property
laws, if applicable."

38
00:02:20,017 --> 00:02:22,656
So what is this worth to Starbucks?

39
00:02:23,169 --> 00:02:27,873
In fiscal 2025, it recognized
breakage worth $200.4

40
00:02:27,873 --> 00:02:31,470
million in its company-operated stores.

41
00:02:31,992 --> 00:02:36,679
Licensed stores added
another $22.0 million.

42
00:02:37,165 --> 00:02:44,169
Together, by our own math, that's
$222.4 million in one year.

43
00:02:44,400 --> 00:02:49,833
The year before, the same
math gives $207.6 million.

44
00:02:50,046 --> 00:02:54,539
For the year before that, $215.0 million.

45
00:02:55,037 --> 00:02:57,576
So this isn't a one-off windfall.

46
00:02:57,953 --> 00:03:01,409
It's a steady stream, year after year.

47
00:03:01,892 --> 00:03:06,733
At the end of that fiscal year,
Starbucks was holding about

48
00:03:06,733 --> 00:03:11,739
$1.75 billion in card balances
and loyalty Stars combined, up

49
00:03:11,739 --> 00:03:15,021
from about $1.72 billion a year earlier.

50
00:03:15,300 --> 00:03:19,720
Not all of that is gift card money,
but it shows how big the pool is.

51
00:03:20,310 --> 00:03:21,710
A big pool.

52
00:03:22,034 --> 00:03:23,928
But look at what flows through it.

53
00:03:24,396 --> 00:03:30,817
In that same year, about $15.2 billion
went onto Starbucks cards and into its

54
00:03:30,817 --> 00:03:35,487
loyalty program, as new cards,
reloads and Stars earned.

55
00:03:36,024 --> 00:03:39,849
And about $15.2 billion
came back out, as card

56
00:03:39,849 --> 00:03:43,259
purchases, Star redemptions and breakage.

57
00:03:43,853 --> 00:03:48,700
In other words, the money flowing out
almost matches the money flowing in.

58
00:03:48,975 --> 00:03:52,577
And breakage is only one
part of that outflow.

59
00:03:54,032 --> 00:03:56,093
Now cross the street to Darden.

60
00:03:56,344 --> 00:04:01,095
Its gift cards also have no expiration
dates, and no dormancy fees.

61
00:04:01,628 --> 00:04:05,516
And Darden doesn't count a gift
card as a sale when you buy it.

62
00:04:05,878 --> 00:04:08,845
It records the sale when
the card is redeemed.

63
00:04:09,179 --> 00:04:12,880
Until then, the money sits
on its books as deferred

64
00:04:12,880 --> 00:04:15,638
revenue: paid for, but not yet earned.

65
00:04:16,148 --> 00:04:20,220
It makes the same kind of forecast
from its own redemption history,

66
00:04:20,220 --> 00:04:23,989
estimating the cards “for which
redemption is remote.” But its

67
00:04:23,989 --> 00:04:26,785
filing adds a detail
that changes the picture.

68
00:04:27,056 --> 00:04:30,095
It spreads breakage over
the expected redemption

69
00:04:30,095 --> 00:04:32,374
period, which is generally 12 years.

70
00:04:32,923 --> 00:04:34,378
Twelve years.

71
00:04:34,653 --> 00:04:37,922
A card bought for a six-year-old's
birthday could still be

72
00:04:37,922 --> 00:04:40,910
inside that forecast at
their high school graduation.

73
00:04:41,276 --> 00:04:43,688
And the pool isn't shrinking.

74
00:04:44,002 --> 00:04:51,448
In fiscal 2026, people loaded $760.2
million onto Darden gift cards.

75
00:04:52,144 --> 00:04:58,848
Redemptions and breakage
together took out $751.9 million.

76
00:04:59,266 --> 00:05:01,518
More went in than came out.

77
00:05:01,726 --> 00:05:06,511
So the pool didn't shrink:
it ended the year at

78
00:05:06,511 --> 00:05:10,481
$636.7 million, up from $628.8 million.

79
00:05:10,810 --> 00:05:16,653
By our math, the balance grew by
$7.9 million in a single year.

80
00:05:17,178 --> 00:05:19,784
Darden is careful to say it's a forecast.

81
00:05:20,091 --> 00:05:24,884
If people redeem differently than it
expects, actual breakage income may differ

82
00:05:24,884 --> 00:05:28,827
from what it recorded, and it
updates its estimates periodically.

83
00:05:30,317 --> 00:05:33,965
Here's what turns this from
bookkeeping into a story.

84
00:05:34,272 --> 00:05:35,991
Breakage isn't counted.

85
00:05:36,284 --> 00:05:37,544
It's predicted.

86
00:05:38,194 --> 00:05:40,626
It even puts a price on being wrong.

87
00:05:41,022 --> 00:05:44,508
Move the breakage-rate
estimate by 50 basis points,

88
00:05:44,508 --> 00:05:48,337
that's half of one percentage
point, and breakage income

89
00:05:48,337 --> 00:05:51,208
shifts by about $3.6 million for the year.

90
00:05:51,860 --> 00:05:53,334
Up or down.

91
00:05:53,676 --> 00:05:57,078
So the money in your drawer
isn't just sitting there.

92
00:05:57,288 --> 00:06:01,945
It's an input to a forecast, and that
forecast moves a company's income.

93
00:06:03,463 --> 00:06:06,668
Now, back to that phrase
about government agencies.

94
00:06:06,954 --> 00:06:09,517
It brings a third player into the story.

95
00:06:09,925 --> 00:06:13,796
In plain English: where
unclaimed-property laws apply, some

96
00:06:13,796 --> 00:06:17,667
unspent money may have to go
to the government instead, and

97
00:06:17,667 --> 00:06:20,291
Starbucks builds that into its estimate.

98
00:06:20,842 --> 00:06:24,162
Darden flags the same
laws as a hard problem.

99
00:06:24,506 --> 00:06:29,726
It lists unclaimed-property laws among
the rules it calls highly complex,

100
00:06:29,726 --> 00:06:34,230
involving many subjective
assumptions, estimates and judgments.

101
00:06:34,815 --> 00:06:37,859
What neither filing tells
us is how much unspent

102
00:06:37,859 --> 00:06:40,585
money actually ends up
with the government.

103
00:06:40,943 --> 00:06:42,561
So we won't guess.

104
00:06:43,934 --> 00:06:49,313
There's also a floor under all of this,
set by a federal rule, Regulation E.

105
00:06:49,783 --> 00:06:54,496
A store gift card can carry an expiration
date only if its money stays valid

106
00:06:54,496 --> 00:06:58,154
for at least five years after
it was issued or last loaded.

107
00:06:58,643 --> 00:07:02,421
And an inactivity fee is
allowed only after a full year

108
00:07:02,421 --> 00:07:05,649
with no activity, and no
more than one a month.

109
00:07:06,116 --> 00:07:09,956
Those rules close off the
quickest ways of draining a card.

110
00:07:10,360 --> 00:07:13,896
What they can't do is protect
a card from its owner.

111
00:07:14,405 --> 00:07:18,329
In that same Bankrate survey,
34 percent of adults said

112
00:07:18,329 --> 00:07:21,611
they'd lost money through
a gift card mistake.

113
00:07:22,103 --> 00:07:25,840
One in five adults said
they'd let a card expire.

114
00:07:26,062 --> 00:07:30,344
That's possible, because not every
card works like Starbucks' or

115
00:07:30,344 --> 00:07:34,426
Darden's, and federal rules do
allow expiry after five years.

116
00:07:34,804 --> 00:07:37,289
Seventeen percent had lost a card.

117
00:07:37,493 --> 00:07:41,532
And 12 percent saw the store go out
of business before they could use it.

118
00:07:41,923 --> 00:07:44,279
The amounts aren't trivial, either.

119
00:07:44,515 --> 00:07:51,171
In Bankrate's survey, people holding
unused value had an average of $244 of it.

120
00:07:52,395 --> 00:07:54,325
So, who keeps the money?

121
00:07:55,099 --> 00:07:58,701
On paper, with cards like
these, you keep the money.

122
00:07:59,100 --> 00:08:01,823
No expiration date, no fees.

123
00:08:02,089 --> 00:08:03,657
The balance waits.

124
00:08:04,296 --> 00:08:08,507
In practice, the company forecasts
how much will never come back,

125
00:08:08,507 --> 00:08:12,653
using years of its own data, and
moves that share into revenue a

126
00:08:12,653 --> 00:08:15,180
little at a time as other people spend.

127
00:08:15,729 --> 00:08:21,032
Where unclaimed-property laws apply, part
of it may go to the government instead.

128
00:08:21,421 --> 00:08:23,806
How much, the filings don't say.

129
00:08:24,477 --> 00:08:28,285
And all of it rests on a
number nobody can know in

130
00:08:28,285 --> 00:08:31,331
advance: how many of us never come back.

131
00:08:31,874 --> 00:08:33,312
The company can estimate.

132
00:08:33,668 --> 00:08:35,532
The government may take a slice.

133
00:08:35,896 --> 00:08:40,725
But only the person holding the card
decides whether that balance becomes a

134
00:08:40,725 --> 00:08:44,652
meal and a coffee, or a line in
someone else's annual report.

135
00:08:45,120 --> 00:08:47,041
So, about that drawer.

136
00:08:47,396 --> 00:08:48,396
Go check it.
